<TopicPage slug="subjects/marine-science/foundational/earth-oceans-and-tides">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the opening lesson of Marine Science, and it assumes no prior science — only that
    you have seen the sea, or a lake, and noticed it moves. Everything technical is built here.
    The lesson is deliberately first, because it establishes the container (the ocean basins),
    the clock (the tides) and the map (the zones marine scientists use) that every later
    lesson — chemistry, currents, ecosystems — works inside.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain how seafloor is created and destroyed, and read the magnetic-stripe evidence that proved it;</li>
    <li>label a cross-section of a continental margin — shelf, slope, rise, abyssal plain — and say why the shelf matters to life;</li>
    <li>distinguish global (eustatic) sea-level change from local sea-level change, and give a cause of each;</li>
    <li>explain why there are two high tides a day, what spring and neap tides are, and why the tide arrives about 50 minutes later each day;</li>
    <li>read a predicted tide curve and say what the two constituent rhythms contribute;</li>
    <li>name the ocean's zones — pelagic and benthic, epipelagic to hadal, neritic and oceanic — and connect each to the physics that defines it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit runs through the lesson: the ocean is studied from three directions at once —
    from above (astronomy), from below (geology) and from within (biology) — and every fact
    here belongs to one of those perspectives.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> one ocean, three perspectives — astronomy above, geology below, biology within — and the
    lesson's order follows them: basins first, then sea level and tides, then the zones that
    classify the water column.
  </p>

  <h2>The container and the clockwork</h2>
  <p>
    The ocean covers 71% of the planet and averages 3.7 km deep — a single, connected body of
    water with geology underneath and astronomy above. That double position is what makes
    marine science its own subject: the sea is an astronomical object (the Moon and Sun raise
    it, and the rising and falling are among the most predictable phenomena in nature), a
    geological feature (water pooled in the low places of a crust that is constantly being
    manufactured and destroyed), and a stack of habitats (light, pressure, temperature and food
    supply change faster with depth than with latitude, so biologists divide the water column
    the way geographers divide continents).
  </p>
  <p>
    Each perspective has its own units, its own evidence and its own surprises — and each is
    one of the syllabus's three checklist topics: ocean basins and plate tectonics, sea level
    and tides, and the classification of the marine environment. We take them in that order.
  </p>

  <h2>Ocean basins and plate tectonics: the container</h2>
  <p>
    The basins exist because Earth's surface recycles itself: new seafloor forms at mid-ocean
    ridges, spreads away, and sinks at trenches. Earth's outer rocky shell is broken into
    roughly a dozen large <strong>plates</strong>, each containing some combination of continent
    and ocean basin, all creeping over the hot, yielding rock beneath at a few centimetres per
    year — about the rate your fingernails grow. Almost all the planet's earthquakes and
    volcanoes are concentrated where plates meet, which is one of the observations that
    revealed the machinery.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="place the plates' pace on the scale" widget="place" config={ocPlatePlace} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Drag the marker to place the plates' speed on the 0–20 cm scale: the answer is about 5
    centimetres per year — fingernail pace — so land inside the tolerance band around it.
    Notice what rides on that slow number: nearly all the planet's earthquakes and volcanoes
    are concentrated where the creeping plates meet.
  </p>
  <p>
    The clinching evidence, assembled in the 1960s, is one of geology's cleanest victories.
    Seafloor <strong>magnetic stripes</strong> — bands of rock magnetised in alternating
    directions, recording Earth's field reversals as the crust cooled and spread — run
    symmetrically either side of every ridge, a bar-code of creation. Core samples confirm the
    stripes are oldest farthest from the ridge; and earthquake foci trace the slabs diving back
    down at the trenches. <strong>Subduction</strong> is the recycling step: where oceanic crust
    (denser, being basalt) meets other crust, it bends down into the mantle, dragging a trench
    with it. That process makes the ocean young relative to the planet — no seafloor older than
    about 200 million years survives, against continents nearly 4 billion — and it explains the
    deepest places, like the Mariana Trench (Challenger Deep, about 11 km), as subduction's
    leftovers.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="rebuild the spreading argument, step by step" widget="order" config={ocSpreadingOrder} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Step the items into order and watch the evidence chain close: cooling rock locks in Earth's
    field at its moment, the spreading crust mirrors it as stripes running symmetrically
    either side of the ridge, and core samples age the stripes outward. Notice the punchline
    item — no seafloor older than about 200 million years survives, against continents
    nearly 4 billion.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the seafloor is just "drowned continent" — the ocean as a
  bowl of water sitting on ordinary land. The two crusts are chemically different rocks, not
  different amounts of water: oceanic crust is young, dense basalt, a few kilometres thick;
  continental crust is old, buoyant granite, tens of kilometres thick. Continents stand high
  and oceans lie low because the rock itself differs — which is also why the seafloor keeps
  getting rebuilt while the continents keep their ancient rocks.</p>
  <p>
    The basin's <em>shape</em> follows the same physics. Around every continent lies a
    <strong>continental margin</strong>: the <em>shelf</em> (a gently sloping underwater
    extension of the continent — narrow off tectonically active coasts like California,
    hundreds of kilometres wide off passive margins like Argentina, and the ocean's richest
    fishing ground because it is shallow enough for light and nutrients), then the
    <em>slope</em> dropping to the <em>rise</em>, built of sediments carried off the shelf by
    gravity flows. Beyond lies the <strong>abyssal plain</strong> — the largest single habitat
    type on Earth, flat because it is blanketed in fine sediment raining down for millions of
    years — interrupted by the <strong>mid-ocean ridge system</strong>, the longest mountain
    chain on the planet (some 65,000 km, mostly submarine), by seamounts (dead volcanoes, some
    coral-capped and biologically rich), and by the trenches.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="pair each margin feature with its character" widget="match" config={ocMarginMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Pair each feature with its signature, working from the coast outward: the gently sloping
    shelf is shallow enough for light and nutrients — the ocean's richest fishing ground —
    then the slope, the sediment-built rise, and the abyssal plain, flat under millions of
    years of settling debris. Notice the ridge in the list: some 65,000 km long, the
    planet's longest mountain chain, mostly submarine.
  </p>

  <h2>Sea level: a moving datum</h2>
  <p>
    Before the tides, a subtler motion: sea level itself is not fixed. <em>Eustatic</em> sea
    level — the global average — moves with the volume of water and the volume of the basins:
    when continents lock up ice ages, sea level drops by well over a hundred metres (at the
    last glacial maximum, about 20,000 years ago, it stood roughly 120–130 m lower, exposing
    land bridges like Beringia and Sunda); when ridge activity is high, the spreading ridges'
    swollen volume displaces water and sea level rises. <em>Local</em> sea level also moves for
    its own reasons — land rebounds after ice sheets melt, sinks where sediments compact or
    groundwater is pumped out (Bangkok, Jakarta), and rises with the ocean's warming (water
    expands) and melting ice.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="file each mover by its scope" widget="sort-buckets" config={ocSeaBuckets} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Sort the movers into the two buckets and the split becomes a habit: eustatic changes ride on
    the ocean's volume — at the last glacial maximum sea level stood roughly 120–130 m lower
    — while local changes ride on the land itself, sinking where sediments compact or
    groundwater is pumped out in cities like Bangkok and Jakarta. Watch the why line: the
    ocean rising and the land moving are different numbers.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is why "sea level rise" is always a two-number story — the ocean rising and the land
    moving — and why tide gauges must be pinned to bedrock to mean anything. For a marine
    scientist, sea level is the shifting boundary condition of every shoreline habitat: the
    intertidal zone, the subject of later units, exists precisely because that boundary moves
    on every timescale from seconds to millennia.
  </p>

  <h2>Tides: the astronomy of the twice-daily breath</h2>
  <p>
    Now the clock. Tides are the ocean responding to gravity that changes with distance, and
    the steps below walk from that single fact to the tide table on your phone.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="why there are two tides a day" widget="equationStep" config={tidesCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the sequence corrects intuition twice. First, the
    surprising step: <em>two</em> bulges, not one — the far-side ocean is left behind, so a
    bulge faces the Moon and another faces away. Second, the everyday step: because Earth spins
    underneath the bulges, most coasts get two high and two low tides a day without the Moon
    ever "coming around twice". Only at step 5 does the real, messy coastline enter — and that
    is where the next section goes.
  </p>
  <p>
    The two-bulge picture is the first surprise and the first correction to intuition. Gravity
    weakens with distance, so the Moon pulls hardest on the near side of Earth and least on the
    far side; the ocean on the near side is pulled toward the Moon, and the far side is
    effectively left behind — so there are <strong>two bulges, not one</strong>, one facing the
    Moon and one opposite. As the planet spins underneath, most coasts pass through both
    bulges — two high tides and two low tides daily. The Sun raises smaller bulges (about 46%
    of the Moon's tidal force, despite its vastly greater mass, because tide-raising force
    falls off with the <em>cube</em> of distance and the Sun is 400 times farther away). At new
    and full moon the solar and lunar bulges align (<strong>spring tides</strong> — the month's
    largest ranges, nothing to do with the season); at quarter moon they fight
    (<strong>neap tides</strong> — the smallest). And the Moon advances on its orbit as Earth
    spins, so the lunar day is 24 hours 50 minutes: each day's tides arrive about 50 minutes
    later than the last, the most practical single fact in any tide table.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="call the Sun's tidal clout" widget="estimate" config={ocSunEstimate} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Estimate the Sun's tidal clout before revealing: its pull on Earth is some 180 times the
    Moon's, yet the answer is about 0.46 times — tide-raising force falls with the cube of
    distance, and the Sun sits 400 times farther away. Read the why twice: the Moon, small
    and near, rules the tides.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> tides are the ocean "sloshing toward the Moon", and the
  bulges are fixed walls of water. The bulge is a steady deformation of the whole water body,
  and what a coastal observer experiences is the <em>passage</em> of water through that
  deformation as Earth rotates — plus, crucially, the real ocean's response, which is a set of
  <strong>resonant basin oscillations</strong>. Water in a basin rocks like water in a bath,
  with a natural period set by basin depth and length; where that natural period matches the
  tidal forcing, the tide is amplified enormously — the Bay of Fundy's 12–16 m range is the
  classic case. The geometry of such basins is also why some coasts (much of the Gulf of
  Mexico, parts of the Pacific) get <em>one</em> tide a day (diurnal) and others almost none,
  while the equilibrium-bulge theory alone would give everyone two. Real tides are the sum of
  dozens of such <em>constituents</em> — astronomical rhythms each written as a sinusoid —
  added together.</p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="true or false — basin resonance" widget="judge" config={ocResonanceJudge} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Work the four statements and read the whys: basin water rocks with a natural period set by
    its depth and length, and where that matches the tidal forcing the tide amplifies
    enormously — the Bay of Fundy's 12–16 m range is the classic case. Watch the false one:
    equilibrium-bulge theory gives every coast two tides, yet much of the Gulf of Mexico
    gets one.
  </p>

  <h2>Tides you can predict with two clocks</h2>
  <p>
    Because tides are astronomical, they are also predictable — the classic tide table is
    little more than the Moon's two rhythms written down. The principal lunar semidiurnal
    constituent runs on a 12.42-hour cycle (the Moon advances on its orbit as Earth spins), and
    the slower diurnal constituent modulates it. Add the two sinusoids and you have a
    serviceable forecast — the panel below draws exactly that sum for 48 hours.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="two tidal constituents added — drag through 48 hours" widget="plotter" config={tideCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the fast ripple is the 12.42-hour lunar semidiurnal
    constituent; the slow drift is the 24.07-hour diurnal constituent, which makes one of each
    day's two high tides taller than the other — a <em>mixed tide</em>, the daily inequality
    visible along much of the US Pacific coast. Count the high-water peaks across the two days
    and confirm the 50-minute drift for yourself: no two successive highs land at the same
    clock time.
  </p>
  <p>
    Real predictions add more constituents (the Sun's, the lunar orbit's ellipticity — perigean
    spring tides — and more), each with an amplitude and phase measured from years of local
    gauge data: tide prediction is harmonic analysis with a maritime purpose, and the same
    method published for nineteenth-century ports still drives today's tables and apps. What
    the method cannot give you is the <em>weather's</em> contribution: storm surge (wind and low
    pressure stacking water against the coast — metres of offset in a hurricane) rides on top
    of the astronomical prediction, which is why the most dangerous flooding usually comes when
    a storm surge lands on a spring high tide.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="fill the two constituent periods" widget="cloze" config={ocClockCloze} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Fill the two blanks and the plot above decodes: the fast ripple is the 12.42-hour lunar
    semidiurnal constituent, the slow drift the 24.07-hour diurnal one — together they make
    one of each day's two highs taller, the mixed tide visible along much of the US Pacific
    coast. Notice that the forecast is only these rhythms added: harmonic analysis with a
    maritime purpose.
  </p>

  <h2>Classification of the marine environment: slicing the water</h2>
  <p>
    Marine biologists slice the ocean along two axes: distance from shore (neritic versus
    oceanic) and depth (pelagic zones from sunlit to hadal, plus the benthic floor). The
    divisions track physics — light dies within about 200 m, pressure climbs one atmosphere
    every ten metres — so each zone is really a different planet with different rules for life.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>pelagic</strong> (open-water) zones, top to bottom: the
    <strong>epipelagic</strong> (0–200 m), where enough light remains for photosynthesis — the
    ocean's farmland, and home of most of its familiar life; the <strong>mesopelagic</strong>
    (200–1,000 m), the twilight zone where light dwindles to nothing — fish here run vertical
    migrations daily (the largest animal migration on Earth, mostly smaller than your hand);
    the <strong>bathypelagic</strong> (1,000–4,000 m), midnight black, cold (about 2–4 °C) and
    fed entirely by the snow of organic debris from above; the <strong>abyssopelagic</strong>
    (4,000–6,000 m), the abyssal plains' water column; and the <strong>hadopelagic</strong>
    (below 6,000 m), the trenches' own realm, named for Hades. The <strong>benthic</strong>
    label covers the sea floor itself — shelf benthos, abyssal ooze, hydrothermal-vent fields —
    and the shore's intertidal band, the most physically punishing benthic habitat of all.
    Horizontally, the <strong>neritic</strong> province lies over the continental shelf, the
    <strong>oceanic</strong> beyond it; the two differ less in depth than in everything depth
    supplies — neritic waters are sunlit, nutrient-fertilised by runoff and shelf mixing, and
    therefore disproportionately productive.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="pair each pelagic zone with its band" widget="match" config={ocZonesMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Pair each zone with its band, working downward: epipelagic 0–200 m — enough light for
    photosynthesis, the ocean's farmland; mesopelagic 200–1,000 m; bathypelagic 1,000–4,000
    m, midnight black near 2–4 °C; then the abyssal water column and the trenches'
    hadopelagic below 6,000 m. Notice the why line: pressure climbs one atmosphere every ten
    metres, so each zone is a different planet with different rules for life.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the deep sea is lifeless, or at least a biological desert.
  It is the opposite in aggregate: with hydrothermal-vent ecosystems running on chemistry
  rather than light (discovered only in 1977), more species than the rainforest in sediment
  samples, and sheer area — the deep benthos covers most of the planet — the deep ocean is
  where most of the biosphere's volume lives. What it lacks is <em>energy</em>, not life; its
  food webs are sparse, slow, and mostly imported.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> a container built by plate tectonics and rebuilt every 200 million
    years, a water line that moves on every timescale, a twice-daily tide with the Moon's
    fingerprints all over it, and a water column sliced into zones by light and pressure. That
    is the stage; the rest of the subject is the play performed on it.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Why do magnetic stripes on the seafloor prove that new crust forms at ridges?</li>
    <li>Give one eustatic cause and one local cause of sea-level change, and say which one a coastal engineer must measure for a specific harbour.</li>
    <li>Why are there two high tides a day rather than one — and why about 50 minutes later each day?</li>
    <li>Spring tides: what aligns, what do you observe, and why is the name misleading?</li>
    <li>Photosynthesis stops below about 200 m. Name the zone above it, the twilight zone below it, and one thing animals in the twilight zone do that shows the connection between the two.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) The stripes record Earth's magnetic field reversals as rock
    cooled at the ridge; they run symmetrically either side of the ridge, and samples get older
    with distance — the only way to draw that picture is crust being born at the ridge and
    spreading outward. (2) Eustatic: ice-age water locked into ice sheets (or thermal expansion
    as the ocean warms). Local: land sinking or rebounding (isostasy, sediment compaction,
    groundwater pumping). For a specific harbour the local number is decisive — which is why
    gauges are pinned to bedrock. (3) Gravity weakens with distance, so the Moon pulls the near
    ocean hard and the far ocean least; the far side is left behind, making two bulges, and the
    spinning Earth passes each coast through both daily. The lunar day is 24 h 50 min because
    the Moon advances along its orbit while Earth spins, so each day's tide is about 50 minutes
    later. (4) The Sun's and Moon's bulges align at new and full moon; the tidal range is the
    month's largest. The name misleads because it has nothing to do with the spring season —
    the tide "springs forth". (5) Epipelagic, then mesopelagic; the twilight-zone fish perform
    the daily vertical migration up into the epipelagic under cover of darkness — the largest
    animal migration on Earth, feeding near the surface and hiding deep by day.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Earth's shell is about a dozen plates creeping a few centimetres per year; seafloor is manufactured at mid-ocean ridges and recycled at subduction trenches.</li>
    <li>Magnetic stripes, sample ages and earthquake foci are the three-line evidence for seafloor spreading; no oceanic crust older than about 200 million years survives.</li>
    <li>Oceanic and continental crust are chemically different rocks — dense young basalt versus buoyant old granite — which is why basins and continents sit where they do.</li>
    <li>Continental margin = shelf (sunlit, rich), slope, rise; beyond it the abyssal plain, ridges, seamounts and trenches.</li>
    <li>Sea level moves globally (ice volume, basin volume) and locally (land rising or sinking) — always a two-number story.</li>
    <li>Tides come from two bulges raised by distance-varying gravity; Earth's spin under them gives most coasts two highs a day, about 50 minutes later each day.</li>
    <li>Spring tides = Sun and Moon aligned (new/full moon); neap = at right angles (quarters). Real tides are sums of constituents, amplified or lopsided by basin resonance — Fundy's 12–16 m is the extreme.</li>
    <li>Marine zones: pelagic (epi-, meso-, bathy-, abyssopelagic, hadopelagic) versus benthic; neritic (over the shelf) versus oceanic. Light and pressure define each zone's rules for life.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    With the container and clockwork established, the next lessons fill it: the seawater
    itself — salinity, density and the chemistry that sets the ocean's circulation — then the
    currents and wind systems that move heat around the planet, and then life, from the
    epipelagic farmland to the vent communities of the deep. Every one of those lessons will
    lean on the zones and the margins you can now label.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three quantitative details pull the lesson together — and a fourth reaches beyond it.
    First, the <strong>tide-raising force scaling</strong>: tidal force goes as the mass of the
    attracting body divided by the <em>cube</em> of its distance — so although the Sun's pull
    on Earth is roughly 180 times the Moon's, its tide-raising force is only about 0.46 of the
    Moon's, an arithmetic worth doing once because it explains why the Moon, small and near,
    rules the tides. Second, the <strong>resonance arithmetic of Fundy</strong>: the Gulf of
    Maine–Bay of Fundy system has a natural seiche period of roughly 13 hours, close to the
    12.42-hour lunar forcing — near-resonance amplifies the tide on every cycle, the same
    physics as pushing a swing at its natural rhythm, and the reason coastal engineering
    (harbours, barrage-tidal power) lives or dies on basin geometry. Third, the
    <strong>basin budget</strong>: the ocean covers 71% of Earth's surface but holds about 97%
    of its water; the remaining 3% is almost all ice (about 1.7%) and groundwater — rivers and
    lakes are a rounding error. That proportion is why ice-sheet behaviour, not rivers, sets
    eustatic sea level on human timescales, and why marine science and climate science have
    become one subject.
  </p>
  <p>
    The deeper current: tides are a conversation with consequences. Tidal friction continually
    brakes Earth's spin and passes the angular momentum to the Moon, whose orbit responds in
    kind — the Moon is already locked in synchronous rotation, showing Earth the same face,
    and the length of our day is measurably growing over geologic time. The clockwork being
    described in this lesson is not fixed scenery; it is an ongoing gravitational negotiation
    between three bodies, and the tides are the visible ledger of it. The same moonlight logic
    returns in later units wherever energy matters — from intertidal ecosystems (built on the
    rising and falling you can now predict) to tidal power, which taps the planet's slow
    spin-down for electricity.
  </p>

